# LibreWolf no-adjust.patch  (Android)
# Owner: LW-M4-02 ("Remove the Adjust install-attribution SDK")
#
# Removes the proprietary Adjust install-attribution SDK, the Google Play
# install-referrer client that feeds it, and every call site of both.
#
#
# WHAT WAS ACTUALLY IN THE APK BEFORE THIS PATCH (measured, not grepped)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read out of ~/lw-m2-04/out-make/apk/fenix-x86_64-debug.apk (the LW-M2-04
# build, versionName 1.0.2634, buildID 20260816204534) by parsing the DEX
# class_defs / type_ids / string_ids tables of all 36 classes*.dex, and by
# `aapt2 dump xmltree --file AndroidManifest.xml` on the same APK:
#
#   * 324 Adjust strings in the dex string table -- the number
#     `./scripts/android-smoke.sh --check-no-adjust` reports (307 for the needle
#     "com/adjust/sdk", 15 for "com.adjust.sdk", 2 for "adjust_config", and 0 --
#     see the note below -- for "com.android.vending.INSTALL_REFERRER").
#   * 358 classes DEFINED in the dex (class_defs, not merely referenced),
#     counted by descriptor prefix:
#         300  Lcom/adjust/sdk/                      the SDK itself
#          12  Lcom/android/installreferrer/         the Play referrer library
#           3  Lcom/google/android/finsky/externalreferrer/
#                                                    its AIDL client, which binds
#                                                    into the Play Store
#          16  Lorg/mozilla/fenix/components/metrics/InstallReferrer*
#          10  Lorg/mozilla/fenix/components/metrics/Adjust*
#           8  Lorg/mozilla/fenix/...Rtamo*
#           4  Lorg/mozilla/fenix/...UTMParams* / MetaParams*
#           2  Lorg/mozilla/fenix/...ConversionEventRecorder*
#           2  ...DistributionAdjustStartupStrategy (+1 referenced-only array type)
#           1  Lorg/mozilla/fenix/...DefaultInstallReferrerClient
#   * the SDK reaches for three optional plugin modules by reflection, and the
#     class names are in the string table: com.adjust.sdk.imei.Util,
#     com.adjust.sdk.huawei.Util, com.adjust.sdk.google.lvl.Util. The first of
#     those reads the device IMEI.
#   * a PREBUILT PROPRIETARY NATIVE LIBRARY, one per ABI:
#         lib/<abi>/libsigner.so   1,274,704 bytes for x86_64, stripped
#     `file` reports "built by NDK r26c ... clang version 17.0.2", i.e. it was
#     NOT produced by this project's pinned NDK r29 toolchain and nothing in the
#     LibreWolf build can reproduce it. Its exported symbols say what it is:
#         Java_com_adjust_sdk_sig_NativeLibHelper_nSign
#         Java_com_adjust_sdk_sig_NativeLibHelper_nOnResume
#     -- Adjust's closed-source request-signing blob. Nobody had noticed it,
#     because it is not a class and no source-tree grep finds it: it rides in
#     inside the adjust-android AAR. It goes with the dependency (measured: the
#     patched APK has 15 native libraries where the baseline had 16, and
#     libsigner.so is the whole difference).
#   * in the MERGED manifest, four entities, only ONE of which is written in
#     Fenix's own AndroidManifest.xml -- the rest arrive through manifest
#     merging, which is exactly the failure mode this task was warned about:
#       <uses-permission com.adjust.preinstall.READ_PERMISSION>        (Fenix)
#       <receiver com.adjust.sdk.AdjustPreinstallReferrerReceiver>     (Fenix)
#           with <action com.attribution.SYSTEM_INSTALLER_REFERRER>
#       <provider com.adjust.sdk.SystemLifecycleContentProvider>       (Adjust AAR)
#           authorities ${applicationId}.adjust-lifecycle-provider
#       <uses-permission
#           com.google.android.finsky.permission.BIND_GET_INSTALL_REFERRER_SERVICE>
#                                                            (installreferrer AAR)
#
# NOTE for anyone reading the smoke harness: there is NO
# `com.android.vending.INSTALL_REFERRER` string in the dex of the stock build and
# no receiver carrying that action in the stock MERGED manifest -- measured, both
# before and after this patch, so that needle of --check-no-adjust scores 0 either
# way. What the version here (adjust-android 5.7.0) actually registers is the
# ContentProvider above plus the Play referrer API; whether earlier versions used
# the classic broadcast receiver is not something this task measured. Two
# consequences: the "INSTALL_REFERRER receiver" named in the acceptance is
# AdjustPreinstallReferrerReceiver (action com.attribution.SYSTEM_INSTALLER_REFERRER)
# together with the Play referrer bind permission, not a com.android.vending
# receiver; and --check-no-adjust does not, and cannot, check the manifest at all,
# so the merged-manifest half of the acceptance had to be verified with aapt2.
# It was. See "HOW THIS WAS VERIFIED".
#
#
# THE FLAG IS ALREADY OFF, AND THAT IS NOT ABSENCE
# ------------------------------------------------
# BuildConfig.ADJUST_TOKEN is null in this configuration -- twice over:
# mobile/android/fenix/app/build.gradle read `${rootDir}/.adjust_token`, which
# does not exist in a LibreWolf tree, and android.buildTypes.debug forced
# ADJUST_TOKEN to null regardless. AdjustMetricsService.start() therefore logged
# "No adjust token defined" and returned early; a captured logcat that upstream
# itself ships as test data shows exactly that line
# (python/mozperftest/mozperftest/tests/data/home_activity.txt:951).
#
# That is not the same as absence, in four measurable ways:
#
#   1. all 307 SDK classes are in the shipped dex either way, i.e. the whole
#      attack surface and the whole third-party licence footprint ship;
#   2. com.adjust.sdk.SystemLifecycleContentProvider is registered in the merged
#      manifest either way (measured), and -- platform property, documented by
#      Android and not separately measured here -- a registered ContentProvider is
#      instantiated by the system during process start, before Application.onCreate
#      returns and so before any token check of ours runs;
#   3. AdjustMetricsService.stop() called Adjust.disable() and
#      Adjust.gdprForgetMe() with no token guard at all;
#   4. `if (Config.channel.isReleased) throw IllegalStateException("No adjust
#      token defined for release build")` means the early return is a
#      *debug-only* path: the shape upstream intends for a release build is a
#      real token, and LW-M4-07 changes the channel.
#
# So the token being empty is a configuration accident, and the acceptance for
# this task is absence. Absence is what this patch delivers.
#
#
# WHY THE PLAY INSTALL-REFERRER CLIENT GOES TOO
# ---------------------------------------------
# Not a judgement call -- removing only the SDK cannot satisfy "no INSTALL_REFERRER
# receiver in the merged manifest" and "no stubs left behind":
#
#   * BIND_GET_INSTALL_REFERRER_SERVICE and IGetInstallReferrerService come from
#     com.android.installreferrer, not from Adjust. Dropping only Adjust leaves
#     the app declaring a permission to bind the Play Store's referrer service
#     and shipping the AIDL stub for it.
#   * every consumer of the referrer response was Adjust:
#       - Settings.isUser{Meta,TikTok,Reddit,XTwitter}Attributed were written by
#         InstallReferrerWorker/InstallReferrerHandlingService and read ONLY by
#         AdjustMetricsService.applyThirdPartySharingSettings, which turned them
#         into Adjust.trackThirdPartySharing() calls for Meta, TikTok, Reddit,
#         X and Google partner IDs;
#       - InstallReferrerHandlingService.start() called
#         distributionIdManager.startAdjustIfSkippingConsentScreen(), whose body
#         is setMarketingTelemetryPreferences() + metricController.start(Marketing);
#         with the Marketing service gone that is a stub that silently enables
#         marketing telemetry;
#       - PlayStoreAttribution / MetaAttribution Glean metrics and the
#         play-store-attribution and adjust-attribution pings had no other source.
#
#
# FILES, AND WHY EACH ONE
# -----------------------
# Dependency declarations
#   gradle/libs.versions.toml
#       drops the `adjust` (com.adjust.sdk:adjust-android 5.7.0) and
#       `installreferrer` (com.android.installreferrer:installreferrer 2.2)
#       version refs and library entries.
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/build.gradle
#       drops `implementation libs.adjust`, `implementation libs.installreferrer`
#       and all three ADJUST_TOKEN buildConfigField sites (the .adjust_token
#       read, its FileNotFoundException fallback, and the debug override).
#
# Manifest
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml
#       drops <uses-permission com.adjust.preinstall.READ_PERMISSION> and the
#       <receiver com.adjust.sdk.AdjustPreinstallReferrerReceiver> block. The
#       Adjust content provider and the Play referrer permission are NOT in this
#       file -- they were merged in from the two AARs and disappear with the
#       dependencies. Verified in the built merged manifest, both directions.
#
# Deleted outright (no stubs, no no-op shims)
#   .../components/metrics/AdjustMetricsService.kt
#   .../components/metrics/AdjustThirdPartySharingController.kt
#   .../components/metrics/ConversionEventRecorder.kt
#       sole caller was AdjustMetricsService.sendGleanEventAndPing; its whole
#       job was recording Adjust conversion events 1..10.
#   .../components/metrics/InstallReferrerHandlingService.kt
#   .../components/metrics/InstallReferrerMetricsService.kt
#   .../components/metrics/InstallReferrerWorker.kt
#       also held UTMParams and MetaParams; see "UTMParams" below.
#   .../components/metrics/InstallReferrerClientWrapper.kt
#   .../components/metrics/DefaultInstallReferrerClient.kt
#   .../components/metrics/RtamoAttributionHandler.kt
#       RTAMO ("return to AMO") detection ran only on the Play install-referrer
#       string: its single caller was
#       OnboardingFragment.startGlean -> handleReferrer(
#           InstallReferrerHandlingService.response). With the referrer channel
#       gone it has no input, so it goes rather than becoming dead code.
#   the five unit-test files that test exactly those classes
#       AdjustMetricsServiceTest, InstallReferrerHandlingServiceTest,
#       InstallReferrerMetricsServiceTest, InstallReferrerWorkerTest,
#       RtamoAttributionHandlerTest.
#   mobile/android/fenix/docs/adjust.md (+ its docs/index.rst toctree entry)
#       upstream documentation of the SDK we no longer ship. Leaving it would
#       have LibreWolf's own docs describe an Adjust integration.
#
# Call sites rewired
#   .../components/Analytics.kt
#       drops AdjustMetricsService and InstallReferrerMetricsService from
#       MetricController.create()'s service list (and `import android.app.Application`,
#       whose only use was the `context as Application` cast for AdjustMetricsService).
#       MetricServiceType.Marketing survives as a category with no service --
#       that is the point, and MetricController already tolerates it.
#   .../HomeActivity.kt
#       drops InstallReferrerHandlingService(applicationContext).start() from
#       createSplashScreenOperation, and with it the now-unused
#       `shouldShowOnboarding` parameter of that private function (Kotlin's
#       UNUSED_PARAMETER is a warning, and this Gradle build sets
#       allWarningsAsErrors for every KotlinCompile task --
#       mobile/android/gradle/plugins/conventions/.../ProjectPlugin.kt:229-238).
#   .../onboarding/OnboardingFragment.kt
#       drops the rtamoAttributionHandler field and the handleReferrer call.
#   .../debugsettings/distributions/DistributionTools.kt
#       drops the debug-drawer line that printed the raw referrer string.
#   .../FenixApplication.kt
#       drops the `else` branch that launched startAdjustIfSkippingConsentScreen().
#   .../components/Components.kt
#       drops `metricController = analytics.metrics` from the DistributionIdManager
#       construction.
#   .../distributions/DistributionIdManager.kt
#       drops startAdjustIfSkippingConsentScreen(), shouldSkipMarketingConsentScreen(),
#       getDistributionAdjustStartupStrategy(), isPartnershipDistribution(),
#       updateDistributionIdFromUtmParams(), the metricController constructor
#       property, the DistributionAdjustStartupStrategy enum, the
#       VIVO_INDIA_UTM_CAMPAIGN constant and the imports that only they used.
#       Every one of these had its only caller inside the removed set.
#   .../distributions/DistributionSettings.kt
#       drops setMarketingTelemetryPreferences() -- interface member and impl.
#       Its only caller was startAdjustIfSkippingConsentScreen().
#   .../messaging/CustomAttributeProvider.kt
#       the five UTM_* names move here as file-private constants.
#   mobile/android/fenix/config/detekt-baseline.xml
#       drops the two baseline entries for the deleted AdjustMetricsService.kt.
#   the three unit tests that constructed the changed types
#       DistributionIdManagerTest (7 tests for removed members, 17
#       `metricController =` arguments), FenixApplicationTest,
#       DistributionSettingsTest.
#
#
# UTMParams: KEPT AS FIVE NAMES, NOT AS A CLASS
# ---------------------------------------------
# UTMParams lived in InstallReferrerWorker.kt. Its parser and its Glean
# recorder had no caller left, but five of its companion constants did:
# CustomAttributeProvider publishes utm_source/utm_medium/utm_campaign/
# utm_content/utm_term as Nimbus targeting attributes, and
# mobile/android/fenix/app/messaging-fenix.fml.yaml:37-38 keys its FUNNEL_PAID /
# FUNNEL_ORGANIC targeting on `adjust_campaign`, which the same object publishes.
# Deleting the names would break the FML at build time; keeping the class would
# leave a parser nothing calls. So the names become file-private constants in
# CustomAttributeProvider and the class goes.
#
# Settings.utm{Source,Medium,Campaign,Term,Content} are deliberately LEFT in
# place. Nothing writes them any more, so they read "" forever, and they are
# still read by RecordedNimbusContext, CustomAttributeProvider and the debug
# drawer. They are inert storage, not a stub; deleting the properties would pull
# in preference_keys.xml and RecordedNimbusContext for no behavioural gain.
#
#
# ONE BEHAVIOURAL REGRESSION THIS PATCH HAD TO FIX
# ------------------------------------------------
# Settings.shouldShowMarketingOnboarding defaulted to `true`, and
# InstallReferrerHandlingService was its ONLY writer: every one of its failure
# paths (FEATURE_NOT_SUPPORTED / SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE / DEVELOPER_ERROR /
# PERMISSION_ERROR / disconnect) set it to false, which is what happens on any
# device without a Play Store answering the referrer bind -- i.e. on every
# device LibreWolf targets. Deleting the writer and leaving the default at
# `true` would have turned the MARKETING_DATA onboarding page ON for the first
# time, because MarketingPageRemovalSupport only ever *removes* that page and
# only when the pref is false. So the default flips to `false` in the same
# change. That is the previous *effective* behaviour, not a new decision, and
# it is one line in Settings.kt.
#
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED (and why), so a checked absence and an overlooked
# one do not look the same
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * mobile/android/android-components/build.gradle:53
#       `exclude group: 'com.adjust.sdk', module: 'adjust-android'` stays. It is
#       a guard against a-c pulling Adjust back in transitively, and it keeps
#       working after the version-catalog entry is gone. Removing it would only
#       remove a defence.
#   * Glean definitions -- app/metrics.yaml (AdjustAttribution.*,
#     PlayStoreAttribution.*, MetaAttribution.*, Metrics.adjust*),
#     app/pings.yaml (adjust-attribution, play-store-attribution),
#     app/tags.yaml. These generate an API that now has no caller; the ping is
#     never submitted because nothing calls submit() any more. metrics.yaml is
#     LW-M4-01's file and removing metric definitions from under it would
#     collide. Flagged for LW-M4-01.
#   * FenixApplication.kt:911-914 still copies Settings.adjustCampaignId /
#     adjustNetwork / adjustAdGroup / adjustCreative into Glean's Metrics.adjust*.
#     Those four prefs now have no writer, so it copies four empty strings.
#     Same reason as above: the fix is deleting the Glean metrics, LW-M4-01.
#   * DefaultDistributionProviderChecker (distributions/DistributionProviderChecker.kt)
#       queries content providers advertising com.attribution.REFERRAL_PROVIDER
#       and reads an `encrypted_data` column. That is the OEM/carrier preinstall
#       partner protocol, not the Adjust SDK, and it feeds only the distribution
#       ID. It stays, but note that this patch removes the
#       com.adjust.preinstall.READ_PERMISSION that let it read a protected
#       provider, so on a partner preload the query now returns null and the
#       distribution degrades to Distribution.DEFAULT -- which is what LibreWolf
#       wants, since LibreWolf has no distribution deals. Whether the whole
#       distribution mechanism should go is LW-M4-07's call, not this patch's.
#   * Event.GrowthData / Event.FirstWeekPostInstall (components/metrics/Event.kt)
#       carry the Adjust event tokens ("xgpcgt", "41hl22", ...). After this patch
#       no MetricsService accepts those events, so GrowthDataWorker and
#       MetricsMiddleware track into nothing. Event is still Fenix's generic
#       event model and GleanMetricsService still tracks Events with a wrapper,
#       so this is Fenix telemetry plumbing, not Adjust: LW-M4-01's territory.
#   * python/mozperftest/.../home_activity.txt is a frozen logcat capture used as
#       python test data. Not code, and not in mobile/.
#   * Settings.rtamoAddonDownloadUrl / rtamoAddonName / rtamoAddonImageUrl and the
#       `if (downloadUrl.isNotBlank())` branch in OnboardingFragment.onFinish that
#       reads them. RtamoAttributionHandler was the only writer, so the branch is
#       now unreachable and the three prefs read "" forever -- the same inert-storage
#       category as Settings.utm* and Settings.adjust* above, and OnboardingFragment
#       is LW-M4-10's file. Measured in the built APK: the 10 RtamoAttributionHandler
#       classes are gone and the only "rtamo" classes left are two Glean-generated
#       ones, GleanMetrics/Addons$RtamoFailedExtra and $RtamoIdentifiedExtra, which
#       come from metrics.yaml (LW-M4-01).
#
#
# ORDERING
# --------
# Exactly one tree file is shared with another patch:
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/HomeActivity.kt,
#   also touched by patches/android/no-nimbus.patch (LW-M4-03 / LW-M4-13).
# In pre-patch line numbers the hunk regions are
#   no-nimbus: [87..93]  (an import)   [419..426] (the initializeTooling call)
#   no-adjust: [112..118] (an import)  [462..468] (the createSplashScreenOperation
#              call site)              [1262..1268] (that function's signature)
#                                      [1275..1284] (the referrer call it made)
# Disjoint, but NOT far apart -- the closest pair is 36 lines (426 -> 462) and
# the next is 19 (93 -> 112). So "they are miles apart" is not the argument.
# The argument is a measurement: both patches were applied to a mini tree holding
# the union of the 36 files they touch, in BOTH orders. Both orders exited 0,
# produced zero .rej files, and `diff -r` reported the two result trees to be
# byte-identical. Neither patch quotes a line the other adds. Recorded in
# scripts/check-patch-order.py's REVIEWED_ORDER_FREE with that evidence.
# This patch is nevertheless listed AFTER no-nimbus.patch in
# assets/patches/android.txt, and the whole list applies in that order under
# `./scripts/check-patchfail.sh --targets=android --use-desktop-tarball`.
#
# No other patch in any list touches any file this patch touches; derived
# mechanically by scripts/check-patch-order.py, which reports one shared-file
# pair for this patch and no ordering constraint.
#
#
# HOW THIS WAS VERIFIED
# ---------------------
# A private copy of the LW-M2-04 source tree (which still carries its built
# obj-x86_64 and the fat GeckoView AAR) got this patch and nothing else, and only
# the Gradle stage was re-run:
#   ./scripts/android-apk.sh --skip-gecko --srcdir ... --aar-dir ~/lw-m2-03/aar
# So the baseline APK and the patched APK differ by this patch alone. Both were
# then measured the same way, from the artefact and never from the sources:
#
#   dex class_defs/type_ids/string_ids of all 36 classes*.dex
#       Adjust + install-referrer classes DEFINED : 358 -> 2
#       the 2 survivors are GleanMetrics/Addons$RtamoFailedExtra and
#       $RtamoIdentifiedExtra, generated from metrics.yaml (LW-M4-01's file)
#       string-table needles of --check-no-adjust  : 324 -> 0
#
#   aapt2 dump xmltree --file AndroidManifest.xml   (the MERGED manifest)
#       receivers 13 -> 12, providers 13 -> 12, uses-permission 37 -> 35, and a
#       diff of the two manifests' element/name lines is EXACTLY the four
#       entities listed at the top of this header and nothing else. No line
#       matching adjust / referrer / com.attribution remains.
#
#   ./scripts/android-smoke.sh --check-no-adjust
#       baseline APK: FAIL, exit 1, "324 Adjust/INSTALL_REFERRER strings"
#       patched  APK: PASS, exit 0
#
#   ./mach gradle fenix:assembleDebug            green (362 s, all four APKs
#                                                debug-signed, per-ABI libxul
#                                                checks passed)
#   ./mach gradle fenix:compileDebugUnitTestKotlin  green
#
#   ./scripts/android-smoke.sh --emulator        all 7 baseline checks PASS on the
#       patched APK (page-load http+https with a validated chain, the L1 WebGL
#       canary drawing and reading back [51,102,153,255], video decoding 5 frames,
#       getUserMedia prompting and rejecting, a WebExtension installing and its
#       content script running, and the pref dump). Removing the SDK does not
#       break startup, which a ContentProvider removal could have.
#
#   a per-entry sha256 diff of the two APKs, which is the cleanest statement of
#   what this patch changes: of 3512 -> 3511 zip entries, exactly 13 classes*.dex
#   and AndroidManifest.xml changed content, exactly one entry disappeared
#   (lib/x86_64/libsigner.so), nothing was added, and lib/x86_64/libxul.so and
#   assets/omni.ja are sha256-IDENTICAL. The Gecko layer is untouched; this is a
#   Fenix-layer change and the artefact proves it.
#
#   Same measurement repeated on fenix-arm64-v8a-debug.apk (the shipping-shaped
#   ABI): same result, 0 for every needle and every class group.
#
# One methodological note worth carrying forward: the FIRST version of this patch
# expressed its 15 deletions the way `diff -urN` writes them -- `+++ b/<path>`
# with a 1970 timestamp -- and the timestamps were then stripped to match the
# style of the other patches in this repository. GNU patch does not read that as
# a deletion: it truncated all 15 files to zero bytes, exited 0, and
# `./scripts/check-patchfail.sh --targets=android` reported "All patches where
# applied successfully". That is the landmine-L5 shape in a new place -- the
# authoritative signal (patch's exit status, LW-M0-11) is 0 for a deletion that
# deleted nothing. The fix is to write `+++ /dev/null` for a deleted file, which
# this patch does, and the check is `find` before/after: 33 files in, 18 out.
